WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.940
The oyster shell to barrier cut tidal volume 30%, leaving a 400 million cubic meter sediment deficit. The gates were built to hold the North Sea out in a storm. The quieter tide that remained set the estuary on a long course of losing the sand under its own flats. This is unintended consequences, episode 163. This is the flood that made a half open barrier look like the careful choice, the models that tracked water and not sand, the slow loss of the flats, the nourishment that

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:33.560
It now stands in for a missing budget, and the ledger that is still open.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:39.060
The 1953 North Sea Flood killed more than 1,800 people in the Netherlands.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.940
Reich's water stat was charged with keeping another disaster of that kind from reaching

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:44.440
the Delta.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:50.040
The Uster Sheldah was one of the last major inlets left open under the original Delta plan.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:55.160
Closing it completely would have destroyed the estuaries' shellfish industry and its role

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.100
as a shipping route.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.660
The compromise, approved in the mid-1970s, was a movable barrier.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:05.380
It would stay open under ordinary conditions and shut against extreme surges.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.340
Hydraulic models at the time showed that partial closure would still allow enough title

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:15.680
exchange to preserve the estuaries' ecological character while meeting the new safety standard.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.560
Decision makers therefore treated the barrier as a technical success.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.640
Flood protection and continued economic use of the basin could sit in the same design.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.760
line. The models treated water volume as the primary variable to be controlled, because

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.460
the 1953 disaster had been measured in water levels and deaths rather than in cubic meters

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:43.778
of sand moved over decades. Construction began in 1976. By 1986 the barrier was fully operational,

00:01:44.258 --> 00:01:48.498
and two compartment dams completed the isolation of the basin from the rest of the delta.

00:01:49.238 --> 00:01:54.258
Early monitoring after closure confirmed what the design calculations had predicted. Peak

00:01:54.258 --> 00:01:57.258
Many current velocities had dropped by roughly 30%.

00:01:57.258 --> 00:02:00.498
The tidal range had fallen by about 12%.

00:02:00.498 --> 00:02:05.698
Proponents pointed to the absence of new storm surge flooding and to the continued operation

00:02:05.698 --> 00:02:09.638
of the oyster and muscle fisheries as proof that the middle way worked.

00:02:10.078 --> 00:02:14.978
A few morphologists inside Reich's water stat had already warned in internal reports

00:02:14.978 --> 00:02:19.978
from the early 1980s that the smaller tidal prism would leave the channels oversized

00:02:19.978 --> 00:02:23.698
and that sediment would have to come from somewhere to restore equilibrium.

00:02:24.198 --> 00:02:26.258
Those warning stayed inside the agency.

00:02:26.818 --> 00:02:29.098
The safety requirement had been met on schedule

00:02:29.098 --> 00:02:31.258
and the visible fisheries remained intact.

00:02:31.858 --> 00:02:37.878
62 concrete piers and 62 steel gates were engineered to withstand a 1 in 4,000 year surge.

00:02:38.298 --> 00:02:42.918
The sediment transport equations that would later govern the flats received less design review.

00:02:43.478 --> 00:02:46.498
Within a few years of closure, the channels began to fill with sand

00:02:46.498 --> 00:02:48.678
that could no longer be replaced from the sea.

00:02:48.678 --> 00:02:51.518
and the surrounding tidal flats started to erode.

00:02:51.698 --> 00:03:00.153
A 1998 paper in hydrobiologia and a 2005 RIKZ report both documented the sediment deficit

00:03:00.153 --> 00:03:05.793
created by the reduced tidal prism, estimated at 400 to 600 million cubic meters.

00:03:06.393 --> 00:03:11.253
Because the barrier blocked most sand import from the Vordelta, the only available sediment

00:03:11.253 --> 00:03:13.253
came from the inter tidal flats themselves.

00:03:14.113 --> 00:03:15.693
Waves kept eroding the plateaus.

00:03:15.693 --> 00:03:19.513
The weakened currents could no longer move that sand back onto the flats.

00:03:20.053 --> 00:03:25.473
Over the first 20 years after 1986, the intertidal area shrank by roughly 10%.

00:03:25.473 --> 00:03:30.833
The elevation of many flats dropped by about 25 centimeters, shortening the time each

00:03:30.833 --> 00:03:33.333
day that the mud flats remained exposed for feeding.

00:03:33.873 --> 00:03:39.693
The 2005 RIKZ report noted that the critical variable for shorebirds was not the absolute

00:03:39.693 --> 00:03:45.033
loss of hectares, but the progressive reduction in daily foraging time, which also altered

00:03:45.033 --> 00:03:48.193
conditions for the benthic organisms the birds relied on.

00:03:48.553 --> 00:03:54.413
A 2011 I-Mera's study found that overall numbers of several weight or species had not yet fallen.

00:03:55.053 --> 00:04:00.413
The underlying habitat change continued. The chain ran from reduced title exchange to slower

00:04:00.413 --> 00:04:06.053
sediment transport to a net export of sand from the flats, all inside the closed system the barrier

00:04:06.053 --> 00:04:13.013
had created. A drop in peak velocity of that size reduces transport capacity by roughly 75 percent.

00:04:13.013 --> 00:04:17.013
according to the power law relationship between velocity and sediment movement.

00:04:17.453 --> 00:04:20.253
The channels could no longer rebuild what the waves removed.

00:04:20.813 --> 00:04:25.813
The compartment dams that sealed the eastern and northern sides prevented any compensatory inflow

00:04:25.813 --> 00:04:29.313
from adjacent basins and locked the sediment budget into deficit.

00:04:29.893 --> 00:04:32.273
No single storm or season produced the change.

00:04:32.833 --> 00:04:37.333
The estuary floor was regrading steadily toward a smaller equilibrium volume.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:42.150
By the mid-2000s, Reich's water stat began active sand nourishment

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:48.930
to offset the ongoing erosion. The first large-scale project placed material on the Galgoplat in 2008.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:54.910
Further placements followed at ShellPoke in 2011, at the Ooster Dam in 2013,

00:04:55.490 --> 00:05:02.210
and at the Roggenplatt in 2019. A new campaign scheduled for 2026 and 2027

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:07.550
will add roughly 3 million cubic meters to the Galgoplat and the Slick Inventon Dortsman.

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:10.890
These interventions slow the loss of intertidal area.

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:16.190
They must be repeated because the imbalance between tidal prism and channel geometry remains.

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:23.790
The 2013 MenER report and the 2026 Rikes Water Stat Technical Summary both treat repeated

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:29.350
nourishment as the current management approach, while Natura 2000 habitat targets remain in force.

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:34.170
Each nourishment episode restores surface elevation for a period measured in years.

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:39.030
The annual sediment shortfall continues at a rate the imported volumes have not yet

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:39.490
matched.

00:05:40.357 --> 00:05:44.897
When the barrier was sized, the design rule counted the volume of water needed for safety

00:05:44.897 --> 00:05:45.657
and navigation.

00:05:46.337 --> 00:05:50.217
It did not count the volume of sediment that same flow had previously carried.

00:05:50.737 --> 00:05:52.797
Safety was met on the schedule that mattered.

00:05:53.337 --> 00:05:55.937
The fisheries that could be seen from the shore still worked.

00:05:56.377 --> 00:05:59.797
The sand budget was not the variable anyone was rewarded for closing.

00:05:59.797 --> 00:06:05.417
A future coastal project that changes exchange area or velocity should include an explicit

00:06:05.417 --> 00:06:08.457
sediment budget calculation before the structure is built.

00:06:09.037 --> 00:06:12.937
The same holds for any intervention that narrows or deepens a title channel.

00:06:13.537 --> 00:06:17.637
The system will seek a new equilibrium, and the material for that equilibrium has to

00:06:17.637 --> 00:06:21.897
be accounted for in advance, not discovered after the flats have already dropped.

00:06:22.477 --> 00:06:26.437
Environmental intelligence is where that kind of unfinished coastal account belongs.

00:06:26.437 --> 00:06:30.237
longs, when a barrier looks complete and the ground beneath it is still moving.

00:06:30.677 --> 00:06:35.377
How might today's plans for managed real alignment or new flood barriers change if every

00:06:35.377 --> 00:06:39.397
design were required to close its own sediment ledger before construction begins?

00:06:40.237 --> 00:06:43.437
We'll leave it there for today, thanks for thinking through this one with me.

